<title>AUSTRALIA: Australian stocks seen down, but well-supported.</title>
<headline>Australian stocks seen down, but well-supported.</headline>
<text>
<p>------------------(Snapshot 8.35 a.m./2235 GMT)----------------</p>
<p>AORD cl	    2706.3    -6.3   DJIA cl	7604.26  -192.25</p>
<p>SPI Sycom cl     2674.0   -45.0   US 30yr cl	6.70    +0.04</p>
<p>10yrbond futs    92.925  -0.065   Gold NY 339.35 Lon fix 338.00</p>
<p>AUD   US$0.7526 (local $0.7511)   CRB Index cl  241.25    +0.73</p>
<p>NIKKEI cl	20436.14  +50.60   FTSE cl	 4575.8    -18.1</p>
<p>S&amp;P500(Globex)   882.00  -16.70   NZSE40 cl    2396.10   -18.77</p>
<p>--------------------------(June 24)----------------------------</p>
<p>*Stocks seen down just one pct on bargain hunters' support after Dow's 2.5 pct slump. &quot;We'll be more sanguine about it. It will be seen as a buying opportunity,&quot; said one dealer.  </p>
<p>*Pre-open bids A$19.45 (-0.05) A$18.87 (-0.03), A$6.41 (-0.01), A$8.74 (unch)</p>
<p>*Dow slumps 192 points, 2nd biggest points fall ever, after Japan PM's comments about potential Japanese sale of U.S. bonds.</p>
<p>*ADRs: News Corp 18-7/8 (+1/8), BHP 29-1/8 (-3/4), WMC 25 3/8 (-1/4), National Australia Bank 70 3/4 (+1 1/4)</p>
<p>*Gold price bounced back US$2.30 to US$339.35, partly on Hashimoto comments that Japanese investors may switch from U.S. bonds to gold. LME copper slumped US$56 a tonne to US$2,511 on speculative selling. -- Sydney Newsroom 61-2 9373-1800</p>
</text>
